{"id":"7059a79b-5772-46a2-b4a4-b87864078490","object":"chat.completion","created":1784802273,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    # validation\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'payload' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case\\\")\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict) or 'matrix' not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing matrix\\\")\\n    matrix = payload['matrix']\\n    if not isinstance(matrix, list) or len(matrix) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Empty matrix\\\")\\n    n_rows = len(matrix)\\n    if not all(isinstance(row, list) for row in matrix):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Each row must be a list\\\")\\n    n_cols = len(matrix[0])\\n    if n_cols == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Matrix has no columns\\\")\\n    if not all(len(row) == n_cols for row in matrix):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Inconsistent row lengths\\\")\\n    import math\\n    doc_freq = [0] * n_cols\\n    for row in matrix:\\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\\n            if val != 0:\\n                doc_freq[j] += 1\\n    idf = [math.log(1 + n_rows / (1 + df)) if df > 0 else math.log(1 + n_rows) for df in doc_freq]\\n    weighted_matrix = []\\n    row_degrees = []\\n    for row in matrix:\\n        weighted_row = []\\n        row_sum = 0.0\\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\\n            w = val * idf[j]\\n            weighted_row.append(w)\\n            row_sum += w\\n        weighted_matrix.append(weighted_row)\\n        row_degrees.append(row_sum)\\n    idf = [round(x, 10) for x in idf]\\n    weighted_matrix = [[round(x, 10) for x in row] for row in weighted_matrix]\\n    row_degrees = [round(x, 10) for x in row_degrees]\\n    return {\\\"idf\\\": idf, \\\"weighted_matrix\\\": weighted_matrix, \\\"row_degrees\\\": row_degrees}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being Python source code for `solve(case)`. The task: \"DATA-SNAP-01\" with payload having a matrix. We need to implement IDF normalization, row normalization? Actually the description: \"Apply the registered sparse IDF scaling, row normalization, self-similarity removal, and degree construction without dense materialization.\" Let's parse.\n\nThe function `solve(case)` receives a case dict with payload: {\"matrix\": list of lists}. Must return a JSON-serializable object with specific fields. Need to validate: nonempty rectangular matrix. Then for each feature (column) count rows with nonzero entry = document frequency. Compute IDF = log(1 + number_of_rows / (1 + document_frequency)). Multiply every entry by its feature IDF and sum weighted rows for degree. Round and return IDF, weighted matrix, and row degrees.\n\nAlso mentions \"row normalization\" and \"self-similarity removal\"? The description: \"self-similarity removal\" likely means remove diagonal? Or something? But from the calculation: \"Multiply every entry by its feature IDF and sum weighted rows for degree.\" That suggests we first compute IDF for each column, then for each entry, multiply by IDF. Then sum across columns for each row to get degree? The degree is sum of weighted entries per row.\n\nBut also \"row normalization\" might be after IDF scaling? Or maybe we need to normalize each row? The instruction: \"Compute IDF as log(1 + number_of_rows / (1 + document_frequency)). Multiply every entry by its feature IDF and sum weighted rows for degree.\" That's it. Also \"Round and return IDF, weighted matrix, and row degrees.\" So return three things: idf (list per column), weighted_matrix (list of lists, same shape but multiplied by IDF), and row_degrees (list per row, sum of weighted entries). Also \"round\" so round to some precision? Not specified, maybe to 10 decimal places? Usually round in SnapATAC2? The fixture values? Not given. We'll round to, say, 10 decimal places? But to be safe, maybe round to 6 decimals? The problem says \"Round and return\". Probably standard rounding to some finite precision, but not specified. We'll use round(x, 10) or just round(x, 10) to avoid floating point issues. But we need to determine from typical SnapATAC2 implementation? Not given.\n\nAlso \"self-similarity removal\": In typical SnapATAC2, self-similarity removal means setting diagonal entries to 0 before computing degree? Or after IDF? The description: \"Apply the registered sparse IDF scaling, row normalization, self-similarity removal, and degree construction without dense materialization.\" The phrase \"self-similarity removal\" likely means we should not consider self-similarity (i.e., diagonal entries) in the degree. But the matrix is not square; it's rows x columns (6 rows, 5 columns). Self-similarity usually refers to adjacency matrix where diagonal is self-loop. Here it's a binary matrix of cells vs features. So \"self-similarity removal\" might not apply. Possibly they mean we should not consider the case where a cell is similar to itself? But since it's a cell-feature matrix, there is no diagonal. Wait: The matrix is given, possibly representing a binary matrix of cells (rows) and features (columns). The degrees are per row, summing weighted entries. There is no diagonal. So maybe self-similarity removal is irrelevant or it means we should not include the entry if row index equals column index? But that doesn't apply. Alternatively, it might refer to the fact that the matrix is binary and we treat each feature as a \"document\", so IDF is computed per feature. Self-similarity removal could mean that we ignore the contribution of a cell's own feature? Not sure.\n\nGiven the explicit formula: \"Compute IDF as log(1 + number_of_rows / (1 + document_frequency)). Multiply every entry by its feature IDF and sum weighted rows for degree.\" That seems straightforward. No mention of self-similarity removal affecting the computation. However, the problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" and \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts: sparse_only, self_similarity_removed, finite_degree, numeric_tolerance.\" This is cryptic. Possibly these are properties of the algorithm that need to be ensured: \"sparse_only\" means we must not materialize dense matrix; we should iterate over nonzero entries. \"self_similarity_removed\" might mean we should not include entries where row index equals column index? But matrix is not square. Or maybe the matrix is square? The examples show 6x5, so rectangular. Possibly the matrix is an adjacency matrix for cells? Not clear.\n\nGiven the lack of further detail, I think the safe interpretation is the straightforward one: for each column, compute document frequency (number of rows with nonzero), compute IDF per column, multiply each entry by that column's IDF to get weighted matrix, then sum across columns per row to get degree. Also round results. Return as dict with keys \"idf\", \"weighted_matrix\", \"row_degrees\". That matches the description: \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So we'll return something like {\"idf\": [...], \"weighted_matrix\": [[...]], \"row_degrees\": [...]}.\n\nAlso need to handle rounding: \"Round and return IDF, weighted matrix, and row degrees.\" Possibly round to 6 decimals, but we'll round to 10 to avoid precision loss. But if the test expects specific rounding, we need to know. Since no rounding specification, we can round to, say, 10 decimal places, but then it might be compared using tolerance? The \"numeric_tolerance\" property suggests there is a tolerance for floating point comparisons. So we should not round too aggressively; maybe round to 10 or 12. Alternatively, we can round to a reasonable number like 10. In SnapATAC2, they often round to 6 decimal places. I'll round to 6 decimal places.\n\nAlso validate: nonempty rectangular matrix. If matrix is empty or rows have inconsistent lengths, raise ValueError.\n\nWe must not mutate input.\n\nWe'll implement as a function that returns a dict.\n\nThe solution must be JSON-encoded as a string. So we'll write the Python code as a string, escape it properly for JSON.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # validation\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'payload' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case\")\n    payload = case['payload']\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict) or 'matrix' not in payload:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing matrix\")\n    matrix = payload['matrix']\n    if not isinstance(matrix, list) or len(matrix) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Empty matrix\")\n    n_rows = len(matrix)\n    if not all(isinstance(row, list) for row in matrix):\n        raise ValueError(\"Each row must be a list\")\n    n_cols = len(matrix[0])\n    if n_cols == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Matrix has no columns\")\n    if not all(len(row) == n_cols for row in matrix):\n        raise ValueError(\"Inconsistent row lengths\")\n    # compute document frequency per column\n    doc_freq = [0] * n_cols\n    for row in matrix:\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\n            if val != 0:\n                doc_freq[j] += 1\n    # compute idf per column: log(1 + n_rows / (1 + doc_freq))\n    import math\n    idf = [math.log(1 + n_rows / (1 + df)) if df > 0 else math.log(1 + n_rows) for df in doc_freq]\n    # compute weighted matrix and row degrees\n    weighted_matrix = []\n    row_degrees = []\n    for row in matrix:\n        weighted_row = []\n        row_sum = 0.0\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\n            w = val * idf[j]\n            weighted_row.append(w)\n            row_sum += w\n        weighted_matrix.append(weighted_row)\n        row_degrees.append(row_sum)\n    # round\n    idf = [round(x, 10) for x in idf]\n    weighted_matrix = [[round(x, 10) for x in row] for row in weighted_matrix]\n    row_degrees = [round(x, 10) for x in row_degrees]\n    return {\"idf\": idf, \"weighted_matrix\": weighted_matrix, \"row_degrees\": row_degrees}\n```\n\nBut we must ensure we only use standard library and no external calls.\n\nNow, escape for JSON. We'll produce the JSON object with key \"implementation\" and the source code as a string.\n\nNote: In the source, need to escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. We'll write it as a single line or use \\n for newlines.\n\nI'll produce a properly escaped JSON string. Use triple quotes? No, we need to embed in JSON string.\n\nBetter to write the Python code as a raw string and then use json.dumps to escape? But we are constructing the JSON manually. I'll write the source with newlines as \\n and escape internal quotes.\n\nWe'll ensure no trailing whitespace or extra characters.\n\nFinal JSON:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # validation\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'payload' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case\\\")\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict) or 'matrix' not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing matrix\\\")\\n    matrix = payload['matrix']\\n    if not isinstance(matrix, list) or len(matrix) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Empty matrix\\\")\\n    n_rows = len(matrix)\\n    if not all(isinstance(row, list) for row in matrix):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Each row must be a list\\\")\\n    n_cols = len(matrix[0])\\n    if n_cols == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Matrix has no columns\\\")\\n    if not all(len(row) == n_cols for row in matrix):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Inconsistent row lengths\\\")\\n    import math\\n    doc_freq = [0] * n_cols\\n    for row in matrix:\\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\\n            if val != 0:\\n                doc_freq[j] += 1\\n    idf = [math.log(1 + n_rows / (1 + df)) if df > 0 else math.log(1 + n_rows) for df in doc_freq]\\n    weighted_matrix = []\\n    row_degrees = []\\n    for row in matrix:\\n        weighted_row = []\\n        row_sum = 0.0\\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\\n            w = val * idf[j]\\n            weighted_row.append(w)\\n            row_sum += w\\n        weighted_matrix.append(weighted_row)\\n        row_degrees.append(row_sum)\\n    idf = [round(x, 10) for x in idf]\\n    weighted_matrix = [[round(x, 10) for x in row] for row in weighted_matrix]\\n    row_degrees = [round(x, 10) for x in row_degrees]\\n    return {\\\"idf\\\": idf, \\\"weighted_matrix\\\": weighted_matrix, \\\"row_degrees\\\": row_degrees}\"}\n\nBut note: The spec says \"Round and return IDF, weighted matrix, and row degrees.\" So we are rounding to 10 decimal places. That might be fine. Also we used math.log, which is natural log? The formula says log, typically natural log in IDF. Yes.\n\nAlso we handled doc_freq==0 case: IDF = log(1 + n_rows) which is log(1 + n_rows / (1+0)) = log(1 + n_rows).\n\nNow, include self-similarity removal? Not done. Possibly we should remove diagonal entries? But matrix is not square. Could be that the matrix is a square adjacency matrix? The examples show 6x5, not square. So no.\n\nAlternatively, the task might be from SnapATAC2 spectral preprocessing: They have a binary matrix (cells x peaks), compute IDF, then compute weighted matrix, then compute degree vector (row sums). They also do \"row normalization\" maybe dividing each row by its degree? But no.\n\nThe description says \"sparse IDF scaling, row normalization, self-similarity removal, and degree construction\". That suggests multiple steps: IDF scaling, then row normalization, then self-similarity removal (maybe set diagonal to zero), then degree construction. But row normalization would be dividing each row by its sum? That would change IDF scaling. Not sure.\n\nGiven the explicit instruction: \"Compute IDF as log(1 + number_of_rows / (1 + document_frequency)). Multiply every entry by its feature IDF and sum weighted rows for degree.\" That seems to be